WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.571
Safe atomic use begins with minimizing shared mutable state.

2
00:00:03.571 --> 00:00:13.492
If data can be immutable, thread-confined, moved through a queue, or protected by ownership transfer, the design may not need an atomic at all.

3
00:00:13.492 --> 00:00:16.269
Use atomics for simple, well-defined coordination.

4
00:00:16.269 --> 00:00:23.809
Flags, counters, and small state transitions should have clear meanings, named states where appropriate, and known readers and writers.

5
00:00:23.809 --> 00:00:28.571
Prefer simple memory ordering unless there is a clear reason not to.

6
00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:32.142
Sequential consistency is often the easiest default to review.

7
00:00:32.142 --> 00:00:36.904
Relaxed ordering and custom acquire-release protocols should be documented and justified.

8
00:00:36.904 --> 00:00:38.888
Use mutexes for compound invariants.

9
00:00:38.888 --> 00:00:48.015
If correctness depends on several fields, a container relationship, ownership handoff, or a transaction-like update, a lock may make the design safer

10
00:00:48.015 --> 00:00:49.205
and more readable.

11
00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:51.983
Review lifetime and shutdown together with synchronization.

12
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:57.935
Atomic visibility does not keep objects alive, stop detached work, or guarantee cleanup by itself.

13
00:00:57.935 --> 00:00:59.522
Finally, test the boundaries.

14
00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:02.300
Exercise state transitions, shutdown, publication, and contention.

15
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:08.649
Atomic code is security-sensitive because small synchronization mistakes can affect integrity, availability, and safe cleanup.

16
00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:14.601
These habits keep atomic operations as precise tools rather than vague promises of thread safety.

17
00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:18.173
They also make maintenance reviews faster and more dependable.

18
00:01:18.173 --> 00:01:19.760
That matters in production.
